{"evidence_sets": [
  {"spans": [{"unit_title": "Abstract", "first_sentence": "Not infrequently a range of pathologies are diagnosed after excluding a thrombosis, often after a period of anticoagulation.Case presentationThis is a report of three patients who presented with a painful swollen leg and were initially treated as a deep vein thrombosis or a baker's cyst, but later diagnosed as a pleomorphic sarcoma, a malignant giant cell tumor of the muscle and a myxoid liposarcoma.", "last_sentence": "A brief review of such similar reports and the relevant literature is presented.ConclusionA painful swollen leg is a common clinical scenario and though rare, tumors must be thought of without any delay, in a duplex negative, low risk deep vein thrombosis situation."}]},
  {"spans": [{"unit_title": "Discussion", "first_sentence": "D-dimer levels as a stand-alone test for the diagnosis of DVT is not recommended as it can be elevated in many other conditions [8].", "last_sentence": "When used along with the clinical risk assessment score, the combination had negative predictive values of 97-100% for a DVT [9,10]."}]},
  {"spans": [{"unit_title": "Conclusion", "first_sentence": "The purpose of this case series is to highlight the need to be vigilant before diagnosing a DVT in low risk patients presenting with a painful swollen calf and make clinicians realize that d-dimer levels alone could be misleading as levels could be moderately elevated in other pathologies.", "last_sentence": "The purpose of this case series is to highlight the need to be vigilant before diagnosing a DVT in low risk patients presenting with a painful swollen calf and make clinicians realize that d-dimer levels alone could be misleading as levels could be moderately elevated in other pathologies."}]}
]}

